      "text": "To find the largest integer that always divides the expression \\((10x+2)(10x+6)(5x+5)\\) when \\(x\\) is an odd number, we will first simplify the expression and then analyze the divisibility properties of the resulting polynomial.\n\n1. **Simplify the Expression:**\n\n   Given the expression:\n   \\[\n   (10x+2)(10x+6)(5x+5)\n   \\]\n\n   Notice that each term in the product can be factored:\n   \\[\n   10x + 2 = 2(5x + 1)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   10x + 6 = 2(5x + 3)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   5x + 5 = 5(x + 1)\n   \\]\n\n   Substitute these factorizations into the original expression:\n   \\[\n   (10x+2)(10x+6)(5x+5) = (2(5x+1))(2(5x+3))(5(x+1))\n   \\]\n   Simplify by combining the factors:\n   \\[\n   = 4 \\cdot 5 \\cdot (5x+1)(5x+3)(x+1)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = 20 \\cdot (5x+1)(5x+3)(x+1)\n   \\]\n\n2. **Analyze the Divisibility:**\n\n   We need to determine the largest integer that always divides this expression for any odd \\(x\\).\n\n   - **Divisibility by 2:**\n     Since \\(x\\) is odd, \\(5x + 1\\) and \\(5x + 3\\) are both even numbers, and both are divisible by 2:\n     \\[\n     5x + 1 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{2} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 5x + 3 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{2}\n     \\]\n     Therefore, both \\(5x+1\\) and \\(5x+3\\) are divisible by 2:\n     \\[\n     (5x+1)(5x+3) \\equiv 1 \\cdot 1 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{2}\n     \\]\n     Thus:\n     \\[\n     (5x+1)(5x+3)(x+1) \\equiv 1 \\cdot (x+1) \\equiv x+1 \\pmod{2}\n     \\]\n     Since \\(x\\) is odd, \\(x+1\\) is even:\n     \\[\n     x+1 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{2}\n     \\]\n     Therefore, \\(x+1\\) is always divisible by 2.\n\n   - **Divisibility by 5:**\n     Since \\(x\\) is odd, let's consider the term \\(5x+5\\):\n     \\[\n     5x + 5 = 5(x + 1)\n     \\]\n     This term is always divisible by 5:\n     \\[\n     5(x+1) \\equiv 0 \\pmod{5}\n     \\]\n\n   - **Divisibility by 10:**\n     We have already shown that \\((5x+1)(5x+3)\\) is even, and since \\(x\\) is odd:\n     \\[\n     (5x+1)(5x+3) \\equiv 1 \\cdot 1 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{2}\n     \\]\n     Therefore, \\((5x+1)(5x+3)\\) is not divisible by 2, but it is always divisible by 5. The product with \\(5(x+1)\\) will always include the factor \\(5\\), so:\n     \\[\n     20 \\cdot (5x+1)(5x+3)(x+1) \\equiv 20 \\pmod{10}\n     \\]\n     Since \\(20 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{10}\\), the expression is always divisible by 10.\n\n3. **Conclusion:**\n\n   The largest integer that always divides the expression \\((10x+2)(10x+6)(5x+5)\\) when \\(x\\) is an odd number is:\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{100}\n   \\]",
